Convert an existing video — Runway-made or not — to a different frame rate (24, 25, 30, 48, 50, 60, 120, 23.98, 29.97, 59.94 fps) using Runway's Enhance Frame Rate. This synthesises the in-between frames for smooth motion at the new rate; it does NOT change the content, resolution, style, or duration of the footage. For those edits use `generate_video`, `edit_video` or `upscale_video`.

CREDIT MODE: Runway account availability is checked against the currently connected workspace.

WHEN TO USE:
- The user needs a clip at a specific delivery frame rate: broadcast (25 or 59.94 fps), an editing timeline (25 or 30 fps), a client spec, or smoother high-frame-rate playback (48, 60, 120 fps). Runway generations are 24 fps, so "make this 30fps", "I need 59.94 for broadcast", "convert to 60fps" all belong here.

WHEN NOT TO USE:
- To raise resolution or sharpen → `upscale_video`.
- To restyle, edit, extend or regenerate the footage → `generate_video` / `edit_video` / `expand_video`.
- To grade into HDR → `convert_video_to_hdr`.
- For a frame rate that is not in the list above: there is no custom fps. Offer the nearest preset instead.

SOURCE VIDEO CONSTRAINTS: at most 300 seconds (5 minutes), up to 4K (3840x2160 in either orientation), aspect ratio at most 4:1. Standard (SDR) sources only — HDR inputs are not supported yet. Cost: 1 credit per 2 seconds of source video (minimum 1 credit), so a 60s clip costs 30 credits. Pass `video.durationSeconds` when known so an over-long clip is rejected before any upload.

TARGET FRAME RATE: `targetFrameRate` is required. Common picks — `25` (PAL broadcast / EU timelines), `29.97` or `30` (NTSC / US timelines, social), `59.94` (US broadcast HFR), `48`, `50`, `60`, `120` (smooth / slow-motion sources). `23.98` and `24` are also available for conforming footage that is not already 24 fps.

AVAILABILITY: paid plans only, and account-gated. If the call comes back saying the feature is not enabled or requires a paid plan, tell the user instead of retrying or switching frame rate.

DEFAULT MODEL (`enhance-frame-rate`): the limits and cost above. Prefer it for broadcast and timeline conversions, and when the user does not name a model.

AI MEDIAKIT MODELS (optional `model` values):
- `mediakit-frame-interpolation` (AI MediaKit Frame Interpolation): pick for smoother motion from a source with a known frame rate, raising it up to 4x (for example 24 to 60 fps). The default model converts between broadcast rates and handles slow-motion presets. Source: up to 300s, at most 4320px on the short side and 7680px on the long side. Output: the same clip at the target frame rate, rounded to 0.1 fps (59.94 becomes 59.9, 29.97 becomes 30). Cost: about 15 credits per minute for a source up to 720p and output up to 30 fps; x2 for a 1080p source, x4 for 2K, x8 for 4K, x16 above 4K, x32 for 8K; x2 above 30 fps and x4 above 60 fps output (a 1080p clip to 60 fps is about 60 credits per minute); each task rounds up to whole credits.
MediaKit needs a paid plan and is not enabled on every workspace: only pass one of these models when `whoami` lists it in `availableVideoToolModels.enhance_frame_rate`. On a model_unavailable result, tell the user and offer the default model. Do not retry the MediaKit model.

